  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule. The rule is enabled and its first run succeeded with no alerts firing.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The Kibana console proxy is turned off here, so I couldn't query the documents directly. I worked from the data view's field list instead. Each document is one web request, with:\n- `timestamp`: the time field\n- `response`: the HTTP status code, stored as text\n- `bytes`: response size\n- `clientip`: client address\n- `url` and `request`: what was asked for\n- `machine.os` and `agent`: client details\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`: origin and destination\n- `extension`, `referer` and `tags`\n\nThere's also a calculated `hour_of_day` field.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-a28d4e2f Web logs overview\"** (id `ea5d5798-8277-4f6b-9aa3-20d2ab847a17`, default time range: last 7 days). It has seven panels, and I confirmed all seven were saved:\n- Single numbers for total requests, average response size, unique visitors and error rate (share of 4xx and 5xx responses)\n- Stacked bar chart of requests over time, split by response code\n- Top 10 requested URLs\n- Requests by operating system\n\nThe save succeeded, but I haven't opened the dashboard in the browser. The error-rate panel and the three charts are built on ES|QL queries, so the first page load is the real check that they draw correctly.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-a28d4e2f 5xx spike\"** (id `42286553-c9e7-4d2e-8528-2cfb4e60999d`):\n- It runs every minute against `kibana_sample_data_logs`, using `timestamp` as the time field.\n- It counts requests with a response code from 500 to 599 over the last 5 minutes and fires when the count is more than 10.\n- Kibana by default skips documents an earlier run already counted. With a 1-minute check over a 5-minute window, that would make the rule undercount, so I turned that setting off and every run now counts the full 5 minutes.\n- It has no notification actions, so when it fires, the alert shows up in Kibana but nobody is notified.",
